-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5a67b95b-ebcb-5bb8-9b9d-986e52e1e701', 'rg10-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Correction$q$, $q$The story was due at six, and at four-thirty Imani Castellanos discovered the problem. Her article for the Ridgeview Courier reported that the school's new solar panels had cut the electric bill in half. A district spokesperson had told her so on the phone, but when she checked the actual bills posted online, the savings looked closer to twenty percent.

She carried her laptop to the editor's desk. Wen Liang, a senior who had run the paper for two years, listened without interrupting. "So the bills contradict what you were told," he said. "Which one are you going to trust?"

"The bills," Imani said, though she hesitated. "But maybe I'm reading them wrong."

"Then verify it," Wen said. "Call the business office. Ask them to walk you through the numbers. Don't prejudge the answer, and don't assume anyone lied. Most mistakes are just mistakes."

The business manager, Mr. Delacroix, turned out to be glad she had called. The fifty percent figure, he explained, was a prediction made before the panels were installed. The real savings had been smaller because the winter had been unusually cloudy. He expected the summer months to do much better.

Imani rewrote her opening paragraph. The new version reported the twenty percent savings, explained why it differed from the earlier forecast, and included Mr. Delacroix's view that the panels would still pay for themselves within twelve years.

Wen read it twice. "This is more judicious," he said. "You didn't make the district look bad, and you didn't pretend the first number was right. You let the evidence decide." Imani thought that was the best verdict she could have hoped for.$q$, 'draft')
