-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d9176f60-6879-5e74-bee5-83307ea28e8d', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Paint the Roofs White$q$, $q$Last July, the city of Marlow Falls recorded eleven days above 95 degrees, the most in its history. On those afternoons, the black tar roofs of our downtown warehouses and apartment blocks soaked up sunlight and radiated heat into the streets long after dark. The city council should require that every flat roof downtown be coated with reflective white paint within five years.

The physics is straightforward. A dark roof absorbs most of the sunlight that strikes it, while a white coating reflects much of that energy back toward the sky. In a two-year trial run by the Marlow Falls Department of Public Works, twelve buildings received reflective coatings. On the hottest days, their roof surfaces measured up to 50 degrees cooler than those of similar uncoated buildings nearby, and the building managers reported cooling bills that fell by an average of 14 percent.

Cooler roofs also mean cooler streets. Researchers at Delmont University found that neighborhoods in which most roofs are reflective can be several degrees cooler at night than neighborhoods covered in dark roofs. For residents without air conditioning, especially older people, a few degrees can make the difference between a restless night and a safe one.

Critics say the requirement would burden property owners. However, a coating costs far less than a new roof, and the savings on cooling would repay most owners within a few summers. The city could also offer small grants to owners who cannot pay up front.

Some people object that white roofs are ugly. Yet nobody standing on the sidewalk can see a flat roof, so appearance is hardly a reason to keep our city hotter than it needs to be.

Finally, everyone I talked to at my neighborhood association meeting agrees that downtown has become unbearable in summer. When an entire room of residents shares a concern, the council has a duty to act.

Marlow Falls cannot change the weather, but it can change the color of its roofs. A requirement is the fastest way to protect the people who live here.$q$, 'draft')
