-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a47257cb-f78c-53a9-b4a7-ef798e743513', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let the Tessaly Run Free$q$, $q$The Ashcombe Dam was built in 1911 to power a textile mill that closed more than sixty years ago. Today the dam generates no electricity, holds back no drinking water and serves no flood control purpose. What it does do is block migrating fish and cost the county money. The county should remove it.

Start with the fish. Records from the Ashcombe mill show that, before the dam was built, river herring ran up the Tessaly each spring in such numbers that farmers used them as fertilizer. Surveys by the state wildlife office now count only a few hundred herring below the dam each year, and none above it. The dam blocks 38 miles of upstream spawning habitat.

Next, consider the money. A county engineering report from last year rated the dam in poor condition and estimated that repairs would cost $4.2 million. Removal, according to the same report, would cost about $3.1 million, and state river restoration grants could cover much of that amount. Repairing the dam means paying more to keep a structure that does nothing for anyone.

Opponents worry that sediment trapped behind the dam will wash downstream and smother the riverbed. This is a fair concern. The engineering report recommends removing the dam in stages over two years, letting the sediment move gradually, and testing it for pollutants before work begins. Similar staged removals on other rivers in the region kept sediment problems short and minor.

Some residents also say they will miss the quiet pond above the dam, where families have picnicked for generations. I understand that attachment. But the pond is shrinking as it fills with silt, and in a few more decades it will be a mudflat whatever the county decides.

Once the dam is gone, visitors will flock to the Tessaly to fish and paddle, and Ashcombe will certainly become the most popular river town in the state. The choice is clear: the county should stop paying to preserve the past and invest in a living river.$q$, 'draft')
