   $q$What was the central claim of Ezra Pollock's pamphlet?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Brennick should refuse to buy shares in either project.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Brennick should fund the canal because canals were proven and cheaper.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Brennick should build a canal and a railroad at the same time.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Brennick should follow its neighbors and fund a railroad.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-counterclaims"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Brennick should fund the canal because canals were proven and cheaper.$q$, $q$Pollock argued that canals "had proven themselves" and cost half as much for shipping. Following the neighbors was Morrow's argument, which Pollock rejected.$q$, 'draft', '4a94ef71-c422-5acd-9e2a-57dc871d959e')
