-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d0776ff5-fcbd-5fcb-8d82-c853d605cca8', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Dim the Glare$q$, $q$The town of Kessling plans to replace all 2,300 of its sodium streetlights with LED fixtures next year. The switch makes sense, since LEDs use far less electricity and last much longer. But the town is about to order the wrong kind. The current plan calls for bright, bluish white lights, and Kessling should instead choose warmer amber LEDs that are shielded to point downward.

The first reason concerns wildlife. Many night-flying insects are drawn to blue-rich light, and studies of moths in several countries have found that they gather far more around bluish lamps than around amber ones. Migrating birds, which navigate partly by the stars, can also be disoriented by bright skyglow above towns.

The second reason concerns people. Blue-rich light at night can delay the body's release of melatonin, a hormone that helps regulate sleep. Residents whose bedroom windows face the street would be exposed to that light every night for the next twenty years.

The third reason is the sky itself. Kessling sits beside Orrin Ridge State Park, one of the few places in our region where the Milky Way is still visible. Each fall the park's star festival draws thousands of visitors who fill our restaurants and motels. Brighter, unshielded lights would wash out the very view they come to see.

The town's engineers argue that whiter light improves visibility for drivers. That claim deserves a fair hearing, but the evidence is mixed. A review by the Calvert Institute for Transportation Safety found that well-designed amber lighting produced no measurable change in nighttime crashes on residential streets. Shielding also reduces glare, which helps drivers see.

Amber LEDs cost slightly more per fixture, about $40 according to the vendor's quote. That is a small price for protecting the night sky, our neighbors' sleep and an event that supports local businesses. The council should revise its order before the contract is signed.$q$, 'draft')
