-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('641390e0-ee28-5d5a-a030-4a90a6d0fe11', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Free Admission Saved Our Museum$q$, $q$Three years ago, the Lindqvist Museum of Art in Port Avery was in trouble. Attendance had fallen for six straight years, and the board was discussing whether to close the east galleries. Then the museum eliminated its $18 admission fee. Last year, attendance reached 142,000, more than double the total from the year before the change. The lesson is plain: every museum in the state should drop its admission fees.

Free admission removes the most obvious barrier between people and art. A family of four that once paid $72 for an afternoon visit can now come for nothing, and visitor surveys at the Lindqvist show that the share of first-time visitors rose from 22 percent to 39 percent after the change.

Critics ask how a museum can survive without ticket revenue. The answer at the Lindqvist was a combination of sources. A local foundation pledged $1.5 million over five years, sales at the gift shop and cafe rose with the crowds, and more visitors chose to become paying members.

It is worth noting that in the same year the fee was dropped, the Lindqvist opened its new waterfront wing and hosted a traveling exhibition of costume designs from film and theater, which newspapers across the region covered. Those events were exciting, but the real story is the price.

Free admission also sends a message. When a museum charges at the door, it tells people that art is a luxury. When it opens its doors freely, it tells them that art belongs to everyone.

Port Avery has shown what is possible. Other museums should follow its lead and let the public in for free.$q$, 'draft')
