-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('770035f2-3fb5-5569-bc1c-d7534f7225a2', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Does Chess Make Students Better at Math?$q$, $q$Last month, the Fernhill School District announced a plan to add a required weekly chess lesson to every fourth through eighth grade classroom. The announcement cited a striking finding: students in the district who belonged to after-school chess clubs scored an average of 11 points higher on the state math test than students who did not. The plan's supporters concluded that chess builds mathematical thinking and that every student should learn it.

The finding is real, but the conclusion does not follow from it as neatly as supporters suggest. Students choose to join chess clubs. Those who do may already enjoy puzzles, have strong number sense or come from families that encourage academic hobbies. Any of these traits could explain both the club membership and the higher scores. The comparison shows that chess players do well in math; it does not show that chess is the reason.

A better test would assign students to chess lessons by chance. Researchers at Okonkwo State University ran such a study in 2019, randomly assigning 640 sixth graders either to weekly chess lessons or to an extra art period for one school year. The chess group improved slightly more in math, but the difference was small and disappeared when the students were tested again a year later.

None of this means chess is worthless. The same study found that students in the chess group reported enjoying school more, and a game that teaches patience and planning has value of its own. The district might reasonably keep its chess clubs or offer lessons as an elective.

But a required weekly lesson takes time from something else, and the district has not said what. If the goal is higher math scores, the evidence suggests that time might be better spent on math itself. Before it changes every schedule in Fernhill, the district should explain what it expects chess to achieve and how it will measure the result.$q$, 'draft')
