-- Passage rg11-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('af3ff9d2-309b-5cb0-bcc9-27bddf2a66eb', 'rg11-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let the Roadsides Bloom$q$, $q$Every summer, crews from the Carrow County highway department mow nearly 1,900 miles of grassy roadside, some of it four or five times a season. The county should cut that schedule sharply, mowing only a narrow safety strip next to the pavement and letting the rest grow into native wildflower meadow.

The case begins with cost. The department spent $2.6 million on roadside mowing last year, mostly on fuel, equipment repairs and overtime. A neighboring county that adopted reduced mowing in 2020 cut its mowing budget by roughly 40 percent in the first three years, according to its published budget reports.

The meadows would also support wildlife. Native flowers such as asters and coneflowers feed butterflies, beetles and other insects, which in turn feed songbirds. In a pilot along a six-mile stretch of Route 9, volunteers counted butterflies twice each summer. The count rose from 12 in the first year to 30 in the second, an increase of 150 percent, which proves that the meadows are reversing the decline of butterflies across the county.

Deep-rooted native plants also hold soil better than short turf grass, which could reduce erosion on steep banks and keep sediment out of nearby streams.

Drivers' safety must come first, and the plan respects that. The strip nearest the road, along with areas around intersections and curves where tall plants could block sight lines, would still be mowed regularly. Nothing in the proposal asks drivers to accept less visibility.

Some residents argue that unmowed roadsides look messy and suggest neglect. Appearance matters to people, and the county could post small signs explaining that the meadows are intentional. Many residents may come to prefer the color and movement of a flowering roadside to a strip of clipped grass.

The county has nothing to lose. It should adopt reduced mowing on all county roads next spring.$q$, 'draft')
