insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d64596a2-95af-5a0c-8d92-4dfada3f99b9', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Point the Lights Down$q$, $q$A column by astronomer Kwame Boateng, published in a county newsletter.

When my grandmother was a girl in this county, she could see the Milky Way from her front porch. She described it to me as a river of spilled sugar across the sky. I have lived here my whole life, and I have never seen it from my own street. I have to drive forty minutes past the last gas station to find a sky dark enough.

Next month, the county will replace its old streetlights with new, brighter fixtures. I support the change in principle. The new lights use less electricity, and safer roads matter to everyone. But the proposed fixtures send a large share of their light sideways and upward, where it does nothing for drivers or walkers and everything to erase the stars.

Some will say that stargazing is a hobby, and that a county has more pressing concerns than the view from a telescope. I understand that argument, and I would make it myself if the cost of fixing the problem were high. It is not. Shielded fixtures, which aim light down at the road, cost about the same as the unshielded ones the county has chosen. They light the pavement just as well. They also waste less energy, because light that escapes into the sky is light we have paid for and thrown away.

Darkness also matters to creatures that cannot attend county meetings. Migrating birds steer partly by the stars and can become confused by glowing towns. Moths circle streetlights until they are exhausted, and the bats and birds that feed on them go hungry.

I am not asking the county to turn off a single light. I am asking it to point them where we need them. If we make that small choice now, a child on a porch here might someday see the same river of sugar that my grandmother saw.$q$, 'draft')
