   $q$Why does the author open and close the column with his grandmother's image of "a river of spilled sugar"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The image reveals that the column is mainly a personal memoir, so the streetlight plan is only a minor side topic.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"The image frames dark skies as something lost across generations, so a modest technical request feels like restoring a shared inheritance.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The image shows that his grandmother opposed the new streetlights, so readers should respect the wishes of older residents.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The image proves scientifically that the sky was darker in the past, so readers must accept his measurements of the problem.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-evidence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The image frames dark skies as something lost across generations, so a modest technical request feels like restoring a shared inheritance.$q$, $q$The memory introduces what the county has lost, and the ending imagines "a child on a porch" seeing it again, giving emotional weight to a request about fixtures. A remembered description is not scientific proof, and most of the column argues about the lighting plan, not his family.$q$, 'draft', 'd64596a2-95af-5a0c-8d92-4dfada3f99b9')
