insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e69ba8a3-448c-550b-80a0-c544a6336df4', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cheer for the Last Runner$q$, $q$A column by cross-country coach Ingrid Solberg, written for her school's newspaper.

At the end of every cross-country meet, there is a moment I have come to treasure. The winners have long since crossed the line. The trophies have been handed out, and some families are already folding their chairs. Then, far down the trail, one more runner appears, red-faced and laboring, still more than two hundred meters from the finish.

What happens next tells you everything about a team.

On some teams, nobody notices. On ours, the athletes who finished long before sprint back to the final stretch and line both sides of the course. They shout the runner's name. They jog alongside, calling out encouragement. By the time that last runner crosses the line, the cheering is louder than it was for the winner.

I have been asked, sometimes politely and sometimes not, why I encourage this. One parent told me that celebrating the slowest finisher sends the wrong message, that it rewards losing. I disagree, and I want to explain why.

Cross-country is a strange sport. Nearly everyone who enters a race will lose it. In a field of two hundred runners, one person wins and one hundred ninety-nine do not. If the only reason to run were to win, almost nobody would bother. Runners keep coming back because the real opponent is the course, the hill at mile two, and the voice inside that says stop. The last runner has fought that voice longer than anyone else out there.

When my team cheers for that runner, they are not rewarding a slow time. They are honoring the decision not to quit, which is the one choice every runner on the team must make in every race. I would rather coach a team that understands that than a team that wins every meet and goes home before the race is over.$q$, 'draft')
