   $q$How does the final sentence strengthen the coach's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It admits that her team loses most meets, so readers should lower their expectations for the season ahead.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It criticizes a specific rival team by name, so readers know exactly which school she is comparing hers to.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It implies a race is not over until everyone finishes, so a team that leaves early seems to have missed what matters most.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It suggests that winning and cheering cannot happen together, so her team must choose between the two goals.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It implies a race is not over until everyone finishes, so a team that leaves early seems to have missed what matters most.$q$, $q$The phrase "goes home before the race is over" redefines the end of a race as the moment the last runner finishes, making a team that only wins seem incomplete. She states a preference, not a rule that winning teams can never cheer.$q$, 'draft', 'e69ba8a3-448c-550b-80a0-c544a6336df4')
