-- Passage rg11-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1370c012-d480-57aa-95d6-66a089871fae', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$To the Audience Who Clapped Too Soon$q$, $q$A letter to a local newspaper from Rafael Medina, conductor of the Riverbend Youth Orchestra.

Last Saturday, at the spring concert of the Riverbend Youth Orchestra, something happened that apparently caused a scandal. After the first movement of the symphony, the audience applauded.

For those unfamiliar with concert customs, this is considered a mistake. A symphony is divided into several movements, and the tradition in many concert halls is to stay silent until the very last note. A letter in this newspaper on Tuesday called the applause "embarrassing" and suggested that printed rules be handed out at the door.

I was the conductor that night, and I would like to respond. I was not embarrassed. I was delighted.

Consider who was in the room. Many of those seats were filled by grandparents, younger siblings, and neighbors who had never attended an orchestra concert in their lives. They had just heard sixty teenagers play a fast, fierce movement with real courage, and when it ended, they did what people naturally do when something moves them. They let the musicians know.

It is worth remembering that the rule of silence is younger than much of the music it governs. Composers in earlier centuries often expected audiences to clap, cheer, and even demand that a movement be played again on the spot. The hush we treat as sacred is a habit, not a law of nature.

I do value silence. There are moments in music when a held breath across a whole hall is more powerful than any sound. My musicians and I will keep working to earn those moments. But I will never ask a first-time listener to sit on their hands out of fear of doing it wrong. A concert hall that makes newcomers feel foolish will not have newcomers for long, and an art form without newcomers is slowly closing its doors.

So to everyone who clapped too soon on Saturday: thank you. Please come back. Clap whenever you like.$q$, 'draft')
