   $q$How does the comparison between a display case and "a ninety-three-year-old's memory" answer the board members who want to wait?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It argues that display cases are worthless, reframing the board's spending as wasteful and suggesting that the members should resign.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"It suggests that the operators should donate their own memories to buy display cases, reframing the project as a fundraiser.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It sets a purchase that can always be delayed beside something that cannot, reframing waiting as a permanent loss rather than a careful choice.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It agrees that a larger grant would be better, reframing the project as something to finish only after the grant arrives.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-concession"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It sets a purchase that can always be delayed beside something that cannot, reframing waiting as a permanent loss rather than a careful choice.$q$, $q$A case "can be bought next year," but the memory "cannot be postponed," so delay becomes irreversible. She concedes the board wants "to do things properly," but she argues against waiting for the grant, not for it.$q$, 'draft', 'e333b01d-90fc-50c6-ae92-812d90d513e0')
