  ('7a285429-1ae2-5776-85cc-000fa86e0528', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Mr. Haddad's line "Glass remembers everything you do to it" is placed at the end of the flashback, just before the story returns to the present. What does this placement accomplish?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It ends the story's conflict, so the final scene in the present simply repeats the lesson.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"It links the past mistake to the present lesson and prepares for his choice to display the bowl.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It reveals that Mr. Haddad is angry, which explains why he refuses to sell her work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It warns Ines before she reheats the bowl, giving her a last chance to avoid the mistake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-sequence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It links the past mistake to the present lesson and prepares for his choice to display the bowl.$q$, $q$Spoken as the bowl goes into the oven, the line bridges the two time frames and anticipates his decision to keep the bowl as a daily reminder. It comes after the reheating, so it cannot be a warning beforehand.$q$, 'draft', 'bcd597d4-e261-5b65-9da7-4aad26879c5e')
