-- Passage rg11-character-setting-plot-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f689672a-fea1-56b4-9f28-07349ce32b88', 'rg11-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Stroke Seat$q$, $q$Before Priya ever saw Danny Vukovic row, she had heard four different stories about him. He had transferred from a school with a national champion crew. He had quit that team after an argument with the coach. He pulled so hard that he once snapped an oar handle in practice. He did not talk to anyone. By the time he arrived at the boathouse on a gray Tuesday in October, Priya had already decided she would not like him.

The river at 5:40 in the morning was flat and silver, with fog resting on it like a lid. Priya climbed into the coxswain's seat at the back of the boat, facing the eight rowers, and Coach Brennan put Danny in the stroke seat, directly in front of her. The stroke sets the rhythm. Every other rower follows him.

"Two thousand meters at race pace," Priya called. "Ready, row."

For the first five hundred meters, she waited for Danny to do something reckless. He did not. When the boat began to wobble because the rowers near the bow were rushing, he shortened his stroke slightly and slowed his slide, and Priya watched the whole crew settle back into his timing. He was not pulling harder than everyone else. He was pulling with them.

At the halfway mark she called for a push, ten hard strokes, and felt the hull lift and run. Danny's face showed nothing except concentration.

Afterward, as they carried the boat up the ramp, she finally asked him about the broken oar.

"That was a bad oar," he said, and for the first time she heard him laugh. "I'm a lot less interesting than people think."

Priya hung her megaphone on its hook. She had a feeling she would be revising more than her race plan.$q$, 'draft')
