-- Passage rg11-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('55ebc6fd-66db-5ce8-b1af-b853af85f1cf', 'rg11-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Heron Who Measured the River$q$, $q$Long ago, on a wide brown river, there lived a heron named Osei who prided himself on knowing exactly how deep the water was at every bend. Each morning he waded out on his long legs, dipped his beak, and announced the measurements to anyone who would listen. The ducks called him the Riverkeeper, and he did not correct them.

One spring, after weeks of rain in the hills, a young otter named Pemba asked Osei whether it was safe to cross at the willow bend with her pups.

"Knee-deep on a heron," Osei said without looking. "I measured it yesterday."

Pemba thanked him, but something in the color of the water made her hesitate. It was darker than usual, and branches she had never seen before were turning slowly in the current. She left the pups on the bank and swam out alone. Halfway across, the river pulled at her so strongly that she caught hold of a floating log and rode it to the far side, a long way downstream.

She walked back along the bank and found Osei on his usual stone.

"Your measurement was right yesterday," she said. "But the river has had a whole night since then."

Osei was offended. He waded out to prove her wrong, and found he could not touch bottom where he had stood the morning before. He returned to his stone and was quiet for a long time.

The next morning the ducks found him at the willow bend before sunrise, measuring. He measured again at noon, and once more at dusk. When they asked why, he said, "A river is not a thing. It is a thing that is happening."

Moral: What was true yesterday must be checked again today.$q$, 'draft')
