   $q$What is the effect of placing the speaker's own stop fifth, after the stops for other passengers?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows the speaker is the least important passenger, so the driver's words are only polite.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It delays the speaker's stop so readers forget why the speaker is riding the bus at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"Having watched him care for others, readers find his encouragement to the speaker believable.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It hides the fact that the speaker is nervous until the very last line of the poem.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Having watched him care for others, readers find his encouragement to the speaker believable.$q$, $q$The earlier stops build a pattern of Mr. Okafor noticing people, so "You look like someone people can count on" feels earned. The crooked name tag in the first stanza already hints at the speaker's new job and nerves.$q$, 'draft', 'd19f30ca-6472-5f8c-920c-5a75b54f3822')
