-- Passage rg11-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('336999ff-fe0f-5352-98d3-9a8e128be4d7', 'rg11-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Counting at First Light$q$, $q$Dao had agreed to the internship because it paid, not because she cared about salamanders. Now it was 4:50 on an April morning, the forest was the color of wet slate, and she was kneeling at the edge of a pool no bigger than a basketball court, holding a clipboard with numb fingers.

Dr. Asante sat on an overturned bucket beside her. She was the kind of scientist who said very little and wrote everything down. On the first day, she had given Dao a single instruction: count what is there, not what you expect.

The pool would be gone by July. It filled each spring with snowmelt and rain and dried to cracked mud by midsummer, which was exactly why spotted salamanders chose it. No fish could survive here to eat their eggs.

Dao swept her flashlight across the water. Egg masses clung to the sunken twigs, pale and cloudy like handfuls of tapioca. She counted thirty-one and wrote it down. Dr. Asante said nothing, and Dao counted again, slower. Thirty-four. Three masses had been hidden under a fallen branch.

"Last year was forty-two," Dr. Asante said, and opened a battered notebook that Dao had never seen her bring before. Inside, in faded pencil and then in ink, were counts from this same pool going back twenty-two years. Some years were high and some were low. The trend, when Dao traced it with her finger, was down.

"Why keep counting if it keeps dropping?" Dao asked.

"Because the next person will need to know exactly how far," Dr. Asante said.

The sun came up while they packed the gear. On the walk out, Dao caught herself scanning a roadside ditch for egg masses. She told herself it was only habit. She also wrote the ditch's location on the back of her hand, in case it held water next spring.$q$, 'draft')
