  ('cc0e94c6-d38c-519d-a7e5-34526fe57e42', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-character-setting-plot'), null, 'medium',
   $q$How does the reverse order of the letters affect the reader's experience of Ilse's story?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The order is confusing on purpose, so readers never learn when the shop opened or closed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The order hides Teo's identity until the end, revealing he was the young man at the bench.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The shop's opening comes first, so readers watch it grow steadily more successful over time.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-sequence"},{"text":"The shop's closing comes first, so the hopeful letter about the lease lands as a moving ending.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The shop's closing comes first, so the hopeful letter about the lease lands as a moving ending.$q$, $q$Readers learn of the sold soldering iron first and end with "it is mine," so the story closes on Ilse's beginning, full of hope. The young man at the bench was someone Teo sent, not Teo himself.$q$, 'draft', '605c577f-e39f-5a04-99e4-c02099866ff2')
