-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4e2a7710-3c30-5026-9fce-daa8a90a602a', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bicycle Craze (1890s)$q$, $q$Text 1: Notes from Main Street (a newspaper column, 1895)

This town has caught bicycle fever, and I fear there is no cure. Every afternoon, clerks, students and grandmothers alike go rattling down Main Street on two thin wheels, ringing their little bells as though they own the road. Last Tuesday I stepped off the curb to buy a newspaper and was very nearly delivered to the next county by a schoolboy who could not steer.

The shopkeepers complain that riders no longer stroll past their windows. The stable owner complains that nobody rents his horses. The doctor, oddly, does not complain at all, since he says the riders have never looked healthier.

I confess that on Saturday I borrowed my nephew's machine to see what the fuss was about. I wobbled, I wavered, and I tipped gently into Mrs. Ferreira's hedge. Yet for one long moment on the river road, with the wind in my face and no horse to feed, I understood. Readers, I remain your skeptic. I simply may be a skeptic who owns a bicycle by summer.

Text 2: The Wheel (a poem, 1895)

I need no horse, no driver, and no fare.
Two wheels and my own legs will take me there.
Past the mill, past the bridge, past the end of town,
Where the river road goes winding down.

My mother says the hills are much too steep.
My uncle says the craze will never keep.
But every morning when the roads are dry,
I pedal out beneath an open sky.

The world was once as wide as I could walk.
Now it is wide as all the farmers' talk
Of places I had only heard them name.
And I have seen them, and come home the same,

Only a little braver than before,
With dust upon my boots and wanting more.$q$, 'draft')
