   $q$Which moment in Text 1 comes closest to the feeling expressed throughout Text 2?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The writer tipping gently into Mrs. Ferreira's front hedge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"The writer stepping off the curb to buy a newspaper downtown.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"},{"text":"The writer's ride on the river road with the wind in his face.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The stable owner complaining that nobody rents his horses.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The writer's ride on the river road with the wind in his face.$q$, $q$On the river road, with "no horse to feed," the columnist briefly feels the same freedom the poem celebrates, and the poem even names "the river road." The fall into the hedge is comic, not joyful.$q$, 'draft', '4e2a7710-3c30-5026-9fce-daa8a90a602a')
