  ('a1a050f1-13c0-5ad3-9684-959c00cce6b2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$What does the boy's question at the end of Text 1 reveal that the speaker of Text 2 cannot know?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The pianist is unhappy with her job and wishes the films had sound, so the music is less sincere than the poem suggests.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The audience prefers the comic scenes to the sad ones, which is why the pianist plays cheerful tunes most of the night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The music is invented on the spot by a pianist watching as closely as the audience, though to listeners it feels perfectly planned.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The pianist secretly saw every film in advance, so the music always matches the action before it happens on screen.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The music is invented on the spot by a pianist watching as closely as the audience, though to listeners it feels perfectly planned.$q$, $q$Ines "had not known" the dog would steal the hat; she was "simply watching," which shows the music is improvised. The poem's listener experiences only seamless music, as when "the rider gallops, so do the keys," and Text 1 says she had no music for most films, so she did not see them in advance.$q$, 'draft', 'aa3d9918-1a09-5b51-9564-9a23ceabc5f9')
