-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9d8c393f-48bf-5db3-a6a5-09caa47c9f27', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Radio Comes Home (1920s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Set on the Shelf (a story, 1924)

The radio arrived at the Lindqvist farm in a wooden crate, packed in straw like a fragile egg. Papa Lindqvist spent an evening stringing the antenna wire from the barn roof to the windmill while his daughter Mirela held the ladder. Grandfather Anton watched from the porch and declared that a box could not possibly talk.

That night the family crowded into the kitchen. At first the speaker gave only crackles and whistles. Then Mirela turned the dial a hair to the left, and a clear voice announced the weather for the next three days: frost on Thursday. Papa slapped the table. If they covered the late tomatoes, they could save them.

Later came a string quartet playing from a hotel hundreds of miles away. Grandfather Anton, who had not heard live music since he was a young man, sat very still. When the last note faded, he cleared his throat and asked whether the box might talk again tomorrow. The nearest town was eleven miles away by muddy road, but for an hour the whole world had fit inside their kitchen.

Text 2: Inning by Inning (a story, 1924)

In the brick building on Carver Street, the tenants knew one another mostly by the sound of their footsteps. Then Mr. Dasgupta on the third floor bought a radio, and on the evening of the city baseball championship he set it on the landing with his door propped open.

Mrs. Oyelaran came up from the second floor to hear the first inning. The Moretti twins followed, then the night watchman who slept all day, then the quiet woman from the top floor who had never said more than good morning to anyone. They sat on the stairs, leaning toward the little speaker as the announcer described each pitch.

When the home team tied the score in the eighth inning, the stairwell erupted. Strangers grabbed one another's sleeves. Mrs. Oyelaran brought down a plate of biscuits, and the watchman told a long story about the games he had played as a boy. The home team lost in the end, but nobody seemed to mind much. The next week, the tenants met on the landing again, though no game was on, simply to hear what the radio might bring.$q$, 'draft')
