-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('770edf92-39d2-5474-8e4f-c7f78e730283', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Pocket Calculators (1970s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Box of Calculators (a story, 1976)

When the district delivered a box of pocket calculators to Room 14, Ms. Abernathy-Reyes left it on her desk, unopened, for a full week. She had taught arithmetic for twenty years, and she worried that a machine that could divide in a blink would let her students forget how division worked.

Her students did not share her worry. They pleaded, bargained and wrote her a formal petition signed by everyone in third period. Finally she opened the box, handed out the calculators and wrote a single problem on the board: the cost of carpeting the classroom floor.

Within seconds, Raul announced an answer of four million dollars. Hana announced eleven cents. Both had pressed the keys correctly; they had simply entered the wrong numbers. Ms. Abernathy-Reyes smiled for the first time all week. "The machine is fast," she said, "but it cannot tell you whether an answer makes sense. That part is still yours." From then on, every calculator answer in Room 14 came with an estimate written beside it.

Text 2: Batteries (a story, 1976)

My grandmother, Lucinda Mbeki, ran a fabric shop for forty years and never once used an adding machine. When I got my first calculator for my birthday, I showed her how it could multiply seven-digit numbers faster than she could say them. She was impressed, and she asked to borrow it for the shop.

For a month she used it for every sale, tapping the keys with one careful finger. Then, on the busiest Saturday of the spring, the batteries died. Customers were lined up at the counter with bolts of cloth. I panicked. My grandmother simply set the calculator aside, picked up a pencil and began working the totals on the back of a receipt, checking each one in her head as fast as the customers could count their money.

That evening I asked her to teach me how she did it. She laughed and said the calculator was a fine helper, but a helper is not the same as knowing. I bought new batteries the next day. I also began practicing sums in my head on the walk to school.$q$, 'draft')
