-- Passage rg11-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fab22f71-36b4-55c1-9d0f-54ad054563db', 'rg11-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Weather by Telegraph$q$, $q$In the 1870s, farmers in the fictional province of Marlow had little warning before a storm. Weather moved across the plains faster than any rider could carry news of it, so a hailstorm that flattened wheat in one valley often struck the next valley a few hours later without anyone there knowing it was coming.

A schoolteacher named Rosa Achterberg believed the problem was not the speed of the weather but the speed of information. The province had recently strung telegraph lines along its railways, and a telegraph message could cross Marlow in minutes. In 1874, Achterberg persuaded the station masters in nine railway towns to record the temperature, wind direction and air pressure at the same hour each morning and to send the numbers to her by telegraph.

At first the reports were simply copied into a notebook. Over the first winter, however, Achterberg noticed a pattern: when air pressure fell sharply in the western towns, a storm usually reached the eastern towns about a day later. She began sending warnings back along the lines, and station masters posted them on boards outside their depots. Farmers who read a warning could bring in cut hay or move animals into barns before the weather arrived.

The success of the warnings created a new problem. Towns without a railway station asked to join, and more observers meant more numbers than one teacher could sort by hand. In 1879 the provincial government took over the network, hired three full-time clerks, and supplied every observer with identical instruments, so that a reading in one town could be compared fairly with a reading in another.

The standard instruments, in turn, made the records valuable for a purpose Achterberg had never intended. Decades later, researchers used the long, consistent record of daily readings to study how Marlow's climate had changed since the 1870s. A system built to save a single season's harvest had become one of the province's most important scientific archives.$q$, 'draft')
