-- Passage rg11-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('da7fbf7e-ec2e-5cef-accb-14b15de0c7e3', 'rg11-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Thirty-Five Seconds$q$, $q$For most of its history, the fictional Harrow Valley High School League played basketball without a shot clock. A team that gained an early lead could pass the ball around the perimeter for minutes at a time, waiting for the defense to give up an easy layup or simply running time off the game clock. In one well-remembered championship in 2011, the final score was 19 to 16, and many fans left before the fourth quarter ended.

After several seasons of shrinking attendance, the league's rules committee, led by a former coach named Daniela Ruiz-Hartmann, voted in 2014 to add a 35-second shot clock. Under the new rule, a team had to attempt a shot that hit the rim within 35 seconds of gaining possession, or it would lose the ball. Supporters predicted higher scores, while some coaches worried that their players were not ready to make quick decisions under pressure.

Both groups turned out to be partly right. Average scores rose by about twelve points per game in the first season, but turnovers also rose, because players rushed passes as the clock wound down. Coaches responded by changing how they practiced. Instead of long drills on set plays, many teams began running short scrimmages with a visible clock, so that players learned to read the defense and act quickly.

These new practice habits changed which players coaches valued. Under the old system, the most important player was often a careful ball handler who could protect a lead. Under the shot clock, a team that depended on one shooter could be stopped by a defense that guarded only that player, so teams needed several players who could score from different spots. The faster pace also tired athletes sooner, and coaches who had once played only six or seven athletes began using nine or ten.

By the third season, turnovers had dropped back to their earlier level while scores stayed high. Attendance at league games recovered, and a survey of players found that most preferred the faster game, even though they described it as more tiring. A single change in the rules had reshaped not only how games looked but also how teams trained and who earned playing time.$q$, 'draft')
