-- Passage rg11-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b5c3a4e7-c9ae-58f6-9a60-95dfa2410554', 'rg11-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cooling a City from the Top Down$q$, $q$On a summer afternoon in the fictional city of Tessaro, the temperature downtown can be four or five degrees higher than in the farm fields just outside the city. Scientists call this pattern an urban heat island. Dark roofs and pavement absorb sunlight during the day and release the stored heat slowly at night, so the city never fully cools off.

In 2016, the Tessaro city council asked engineers at the local university to find an inexpensive way to lower summer temperatures. The team, led by Dr. Mei-Lin Abernathy, focused on roofs, which cover nearly a quarter of the city's surface. A dark roof can reach 70 degrees Celsius on a sunny day, while a roof coated with bright white paint reflects most of the sunlight and stays far cooler.

The team began with a pilot project on forty buildings in the Garnet Street neighborhood. Within one summer, the top floors of those buildings were measurably cooler, and residents ran their air conditioners less often. Lower demand for electricity on the hottest afternoons also eased the strain on the city's power grid, which had suffered two blackouts during heat waves in previous years.

The pilot's results persuaded the council to offer grants that covered half the cost of coating a roof. Over the next five years, more than three thousand buildings took part. Abernathy's team found that once enough roofs in a neighborhood were coated, the air temperature on the street itself dropped slightly, an effect that no single building could produce on its own.

The project also revealed a limit. In winter, white roofs reflected sunlight that would otherwise have helped warm buildings, so heating costs rose a little in the coldest months. Because Tessaro's summers are long and its winters mild, the summer savings were much larger than the winter costs. Abernathy's final report noted, however, that a city with long, harsh winters might reach a different conclusion, and it recommended that each city weigh both seasons before copying Tessaro's approach.$q$, 'draft')
