-- Passage rg11-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fb7b5da7-5f6a-5c93-a4a1-7ed86632ecd3', 'rg11-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Orchestra That Went Outside$q$, $q$By 2015, the Halvorsen Chamber Orchestra, a small professional ensemble in the fictional river city of Pell, was in trouble. Its concert hall seated eight hundred people, but most performances drew fewer than three hundred, and the average age of its audience was over sixty. Ticket sales no longer covered the cost of renting the hall, and the orchestra's board warned that it might have to shorten its season.

The orchestra's new director, Kwame Adjei-Lindqvist, proposed an experiment that some board members considered risky. Instead of asking new listeners to come to the hall, the orchestra would go to them. During the following spring, groups of four to eight musicians performed free, forty-minute concerts in places where people already gathered: the central train station at rush hour, branch libraries on weekend mornings and the lobby of a children's hospital.

The early audiences were small and often distracted, but the musicians learned quickly. They found that people stayed longer when a player briefly explained a piece before performing it, so short spoken introductions became part of every program. Commuters who had stopped for one piece began returning on the same weekday, and several libraries asked for a regular monthly concert.

The neighborhood series never paid for itself, but it changed the main season in ways the board had not expected. Many listeners who first heard the orchestra in a station or library later bought tickets to a full concert, often bringing their families. To welcome them, the orchestra added spoken introductions to its hall concerts as well and began offering low-priced seats for students. By 2019, average attendance at hall performances had risen to nearly six hundred, and the audience included far more people under forty.

Some longtime subscribers at first objected to the introductions, saying that they interrupted the music. The orchestra kept them short and placed them only before less familiar works. Within two seasons, a subscriber survey found that most longtime listeners valued them too, and the complaints largely faded.$q$, 'draft')
