   $q$According to paragraph 1, why did villages along the canal grow after 1832?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Canal traffic supported new inns, stables and boatyards at nearly every lock.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Tourists came to rent boats and stay in guesthouses along the canal's route.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-sequence"},{"text":"Coal mines opened in each village once the canal reached the upper valley.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The railway brought workers to build homes along the valley's new tracks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-sequence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Canal traffic supported new inns, stables and boatyards at nearly every lock.$q$, $q$Paragraph 1 ties village growth to the inns, stables and boatyards that "appeared at nearly every lock" to serve canal traffic. Boat rentals and guesthouses belong to the canal's second life, more than a century later.$q$, 'draft', '7bcd10dd-2dd1-52ac-9b4b-443eefb87944')
