-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('534e4096-63f9-5212-9ee7-c4578e9c3c18', 'rg11-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Archive Beneath the Snow$q$, $q$Most snowfall is ephemeral. A storm blankets a hillside in the evening, and by the following afternoon the sun has erased nearly every trace of it. On the high ice sheets near the poles, however, the snow that falls each winter does not melt away. It lingers, buried under the next year's snow, and the next, until the weight of the layers above compresses it into dense, glassy ice.

Over thousands of years, this process builds a frozen record of the planet's past. When researchers drill into an ice sheet, they extract long cylinders called ice cores. Each core is stratified: it is divided into distinct bands, one stacked on another, much like the rings of a tree turned on their side. A pale band may mark a snowy winter, while a darker one may hold dust blown in during a dry summer.

The most valuable evidence is too small to see. As snow turns to ice, it traps tiny bubbles of air. Those bubbles are samples of the atmosphere from the moment they sealed shut. By measuring the gases inside, scientists can reconstruct the composition of air that people breathed long before anyone kept written weather records.

Reading an ice core is painstaking work. A single meter of ice may take a team several days to cut, label, and analyze, and one careless slice can contaminate a sample beyond use. For that reason, laboratories store cores in freezers held far below zero, and researchers handle them with gloves and sterilized tools.

The findings are rarely dramatic on their own. A single core offers only a tenuous claim about the past, since local winds or a brief melting event might have distorted its layers. When cores drilled hundreds of kilometers apart show the same pattern, though, the evidence becomes far harder to dismiss. Scientists say the separate cores corroborate one another, each one confirming what the others suggest.$q$, 'draft')
