-- Passage rg11-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5427689b-ca11-5ac5-b4d7-a69744413f67', 'rg11-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Some Blocks Run Hotter$q$, $q$On a summer afternoon, two neighborhoods in the same city can feel like different climates. In one, tall trees shade the sidewalks and a breeze moves between low buildings. In another, a few blocks away, asphalt and dark rooftops are ubiquitous; they cover nearly every surface in sight, and the air shimmers above them. Researchers who measure these disparate conditions have found that temperatures on such blocks can differ by several degrees, even though the same sun shines on both.

The cause is largely a matter of materials. Dark pavement and roofing absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly after sunset, so the hottest streets stay warm long into the night. Traffic, air conditioners, and idling engines add their own heat, which can exacerbate the problem. On a day that is already uncomfortable, this extra warmth can push temperatures into a dangerous range for older residents and young children.

Cities have tried several strategies to mitigate the effect. Planting street trees is one of the most popular, because the leaves shade pavement and release moisture that cools the surrounding air. Some cities paint roofs white or pale gray so that they reflect sunlight instead of soaking it up. Others replace parking lots with pavement that lets rainwater drain through, which keeps the ground cooler.

None of these solutions works instantly. A newly planted tree may need a decade to grow large enough to cast meaningful shade, and a reflective roof helps mainly the building beneath it until many neighbors adopt the same idea. Planners therefore describe the work as incremental: progress arrives in small steps, block by block, rather than in one sweeping change.

Residents can contribute as well. Neighborhood groups in several cities now track temperatures with inexpensive sensors and share the readings with local officials, giving planners a clearer picture of which streets need help first.$q$, 'draft')
