-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('23cfc83d-ad00-5c09-844e-bd91e4f2b73b', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the Air on Tree Bark$q$, $q$Lichens look like flaky crusts of gray, green or orange paint on tree bark and stone, but each one is a partnership between a fungus and a tiny organism that makes food from sunlight. Because lichens have no roots and no protective outer layer, they absorb water and nutrients straight from rain and air. That same openness makes many species sensitive to pollutants, especially compounds of nitrogen and sulfur that come from burning fuel.

In a survey of the city of Callowmere, ecologist Priya Nandakumar and a team of volunteer students recorded every lichen species growing on the trunks of 240 street trees. They chose trees of the same species and similar age so that differences in bark would not distort the results. Along the inner ring road, where traffic is heaviest, the team found an average of three lichen species per tree, almost all of them a tolerant yellow type. In parks more than a kilometer from major roads, the average rose to eleven species, including several delicate, leafy forms that were absent near the ring road.

The team then compared its findings with readings from the city's four air quality stations. Trees near stations that reported higher nitrogen dioxide levels tended to carry fewer species. Nandakumar has described the lichens as a low-cost network of monitors that never needs batteries. A single trained volunteer can survey dozens of trees in a day, while each electronic station costs more than the city's entire annual budget for tree care.

The survey has limits. Lichens grow slowly, so a tree's lichen community reflects years of conditions rather than last week's air. Shade, humidity and the dust kicked up by street sweepers may also affect which species survive, and the team did not measure those factors. Nandakumar plans to repeat the survey in five years to see whether a new low-emission zone around the city center changes the pattern.$q$, 'draft')
