-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8800749b-2ae2-5f2d-8c44-13606e370353', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Ice Fields of Lake Orrin$q$, $q$Before electric refrigerators became common, many households kept food cold with blocks of ice cut from frozen lakes. In the town of Brenford, ice harvesting on Lake Orrin grew from a small winter sideline into the town's largest employer for nearly fifty years.

Harvesting began once the ice was at least twelve inches thick. Workers first scraped away snow, then used horse-drawn plows with steel teeth to score the surface into a grid. Crews finished the cuts with long hand saws and floated the blocks along an open channel to a ramp, where a conveyor lifted them into storage houses. The houses had double walls packed with sawdust, which slowed melting so well that ice cut in January could be sold in August. Company ledgers from 1889 record that roughly a third of each winter's harvest was lost to melting before it was sold, a figure managers considered acceptable.

Brenford's ice traveled by rail to cities as far as three hundred miles away. The work paid better than most winter jobs in the region, and farmers who had little to do between harvests often joined the crews. Local school records show that attendance among older boys dropped sharply every January.

The trade declined quickly after 1915. Factories that froze water using machines could produce ice year round, close to the cities that bought it, and their ice was free of the leaves and sediment that sometimes appeared in lake ice. Health inspectors in several cities had begun warning that natural ice drawn from ponds near farms could carry contamination, though Lake Orrin's water was tested less often than that of other sources. By 1925 the last of Brenford's ice houses had closed. Today only stone foundations remain along the shore, and a local historical society is gathering photographs to learn how many people the industry once employed at its peak.$q$, 'draft')
