-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('914ee808-4690-58a9-b0a8-2131e39903e0', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Searching the Old Papers$q$, $q$When the Harlow County Library decided to put its collection of local newspapers online, the staff expected the hardest part to be scanning. The collection covers 1851 to 1979 and fills more than four thousand bound volumes, some so brittle that their pages crack when turned. Archivist Tomasz Wierzbicki and two assistants built a cradle that holds each book open at a gentle angle while an overhead camera photographs the pages, and they finished the scanning in fourteen months.

The real challenge came next. A photograph of a page is just an image, so a search for a family name or a street will not find anything inside it. To make the text searchable, the library ran every image through optical character recognition software, which tries to identify each letter by its shape. On clean pages from the 1960s and 1970s, the software matched a human transcriber on about ninety-eight of every hundred words. On pages from before 1880, where the ink had faded or bled through from the other side, accuracy fell to roughly seventy words in a hundred. Older typefaces caused further trouble: the software often read a long, curved letter s, used in some early printing, as an f.

The library posted the results anyway, with a note on its website explaining that searches of nineteenth-century issues will miss some matches. It also invited volunteers to correct the text one article at a time. In the first year, about six hundred volunteers corrected roughly nine thousand articles. Most corrections were made to articles about local families and businesses, while long columns of shipping schedules and grain prices were rarely touched.

Wierzbicki says the project changed how he thinks about the word "online." A newspaper that appears in search results is not necessarily one whose contents can be found, he notes, and a researcher who gets no results for a name in 1860 should not assume that the name was never printed.$q$, 'draft')
