-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d6472840-821c-5e15-81d4-1854bb9fc0dc', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Figure Beneath the Orchard$q$, $q$When conservators at the Linden Hall Museum began cleaning a landscape called The Orchard at Vell, they expected to remove a century of grime and yellowed varnish. The painting, completed around 1840 by the little-known artist Halvard Ensing, shows rows of pear trees and a stone wall under a pale sky. What the conservators did not expect to find was a person.

Infrared imaging, which can pass through some layers of paint and reveal carbon-based drawing materials beneath, showed a sketched figure of a woman standing beside the wall and holding a basket. The drawing was detailed, with folds in her sleeves and a carefully shaded face. Yet no trace of her appears in the finished painting. Instead, Ensing painted more leaves and a section of wall over the spot where she would have stood.

Lead conservator Mariana Quispe noted that the paint covering the figure matches the rest of the picture in its chemistry and in the way it cracked as it aged. That evidence suggests the change was made by Ensing himself, or at least during his time, rather than by a later owner. It does not reveal why the figure was removed. Ensing's surviving letters, which mention prices for paint and visits to the orchard, say nothing about this picture. Some staff members have proposed that a buyer requested a landscape without people. Others point out that two other paintings by Ensing in the museum's collection also contain no figures, which could reflect his own preference.

The museum has decided not to remove the top layers to uncover the drawing, since doing so would destroy paint that Ensing applied himself. Instead, a printed infrared image now hangs beside the painting, so visitors can see both versions of the scene at once.$q$, 'draft')
