-- Passage rg11-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2e1a92dc-63ae-5d19-8a5f-48f18cf020e7', 'rg11-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Following the Amber Gliders$q$, $q$Each autumn, observers at Sorrel Point, a narrow strip of land along a large lake, count thousands of amber gliders, a dragonfly species, flying south. For decades no one knew where the insects went or whether the same individuals ever returned. A dragonfly weighs less than a paper clip, far too little to carry the tracking devices used on birds.

In 2021, a team led by entomologist Kwame Asante-Bell tested radio tags weighing about three hundred milligrams, which were glued to the underside of each dragonfly's body. Before releasing any tagged insects, the team filmed forty tagged and forty untagged gliders in a large mesh enclosure. The two groups flew at nearly identical speeds and spent similar amounts of time resting, so the researchers concluded that the tags were unlikely to change the insects' behavior very much.

Over three autumns the team tagged 214 gliders and followed them with receivers mounted on towers and on a small airplane. The insects did not fly every day. Most traveled only on days after a cold front had passed, when winds blew from the north, and they rested for two or three days between flights. The longest single trip recorded was 130 kilometers in one day. None of the tagged dragonflies was ever detected flying north again in spring.

Asante-Bell's team suspects that the gliders that leave in autumn lay eggs in the south and that their offspring, or the offspring of those offspring, make the return journey. The tag batteries, however, last only about two weeks, and the team has not yet found the southern breeding grounds. Until it does, he says, the shape of the full migration remains a reasonable guess rather than a finding.$q$, 'draft')
