   $q$Crews coat roofs with a material that "reflects sunlight instead of soaking it up." What does "soaking it up" mean here?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Cooling the air through evaporation","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"Taking in and holding the sun's heat","is_correct":true},{"text":"Getting wet when rain falls on the roof","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Bouncing light back toward the sky above","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-opposite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Taking in and holding the sun's heat$q$, $q$"Soaking it up" is a figurative way to say absorbing, matching the earlier point that dark roofing "absorb[s] sunlight" and stores heat. Bouncing light back is what reflecting means, which is the opposite of soaking it up.$q$, 'draft', 'cdce2745-3cc2-59f5-9849-e7b32611a803')
