values
  ('9711f917-2637-5674-8beb-f5406f80b913', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author writes that researchers "stress" that benefits are not shared evenly. How does "stress" differ from a word with a similar meaning, such as "mention"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Stress\" suggests that the researchers disagree with each other about fairness.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"\"Stress\" and \"mention\" both suggest a quick remark that readers can skip over.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Stress\" shows that the researchers feel anxious and worried about their work.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-meaning-of-word"},{"text":"\"Stress\" signals that researchers insist the point is important, not a side note.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Stress" signals that researchers insist the point is important, not a side note.$q$, $q$To "stress" a point is to emphasize it, which fits the final paragraph's claim that fairness is a central question; "mention" would make it sound minor. The tempting "anxious" reading uses a different meaning of the word that does not fit researchers reporting a finding.$q$, 'draft', 'cdce2745-3cc2-59f5-9849-e7b32611a803')
