values
  ('a51c0c02-90d6-5450-9474-cea73270bfca', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The author calls the work "painstaking." How does this word differ from a near synonym such as "tedious"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Painstaking\" suggests the work is rushed, while \"tedious\" is slow.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Painstaking\" suggests the work is dull, while \"tedious\" praises it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-opposite"},{"text":"\"Painstaking\" means the work physically hurts the scientists.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"word-part-literal"},{"text":"\"Painstaking\" stresses great care, while \"tedious\" stresses boredom.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Painstaking" stresses great care, while "tedious" stresses boredom.$q$, $q$The examples of slicing, labeling and freezing every section show careful attention, which is what "painstaking" emphasizes; "tedious" would focus on boredom instead. The word contains "pain," but it does not mean the work causes injury.$q$, 'draft', '749dcbcc-aaba-5b63-9630-c346625f1c23')
