  ('78ecc702-5765-5e17-8c32-9d419f9c2b00', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Amara "shook her head politely and declined" the draw. Why is "declined" a better fit than "refused," a word with a similar dictionary meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Declined\" means she accepted the offer, while \"refused\" means she said no.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-meaning-of-word"},{"text":"\"Declined\" sounds courteous, matching her polite manner, while \"refused\" sounds blunt.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Declined\" suggests she was unsure, while \"refused\" means she was certain.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"\"Declined\" and \"refused\" carry the same tone, so either fits the moment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Declined" sounds courteous, matching her polite manner, while "refused" sounds blunt.$q$, $q$Both words mean saying no, but "declined" carries a polite, respectful tone that fits "shook her head politely," while "refused" can sound harsh or defiant. Her careful thinking shows she was not unsure, so the uncertainty reading is unsupported.$q$, 'draft', '936952f3-ff9f-55d2-b339-31906970cce9')
