-- Passage rg11-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2c939315-9602-56db-a4ae-170c85dba003', 'rg11-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Links Break$q$, $q$Anyone who has clicked an old link and landed on an error page has met a problem that researchers call link rot. Web pages move, websites shut down, and addresses that once led somewhere useful now lead nowhere. Studies of academic papers have found that many of the links in their reference lists stop working within a decade.

The term link rot is a deliberate metaphor. Rot suggests something slow, quiet, and natural, the way fruit spoils on a counter without anyone noticing until it is too late. That is how most links fail. No one decides to break them. A website owner reorganizes pages, a small organization stops paying for its web address, or a server is shut off, and the link simply decays.

The internet can seem like a place where nothing is ever forgotten. Embarrassing photos resurface years later, and old posts follow people around. Yet the same internet is remarkably forgetful about the sources that scholars, journalists, and students depend on. It remembers what people wish it would forget and forgets what people need it to remember.

Librarians and archivists have responded by building web archives, which save copies of pages at particular moments. When a citation points to an archived copy, a reader years later can still see what the author saw. Some journals now ask writers to archive every source they cite.

None of these solutions is perfect. Archives cannot capture everything, and some pages change so often that a single snapshot can mislead. Still, the effort reflects a simple idea: a source that cannot be found is, for most readers, a source that does not exist.$q$, 'draft')
