   $q$The author says that the link "simply decays." Why does "decays" fit the passage better than "breaks"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Decays\" suggests a sudden crash caused by a single person.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-opposite"},{"text":"\"Decays\" means the link can be fixed, while \"breaks\" cannot.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"\"Decays\" suggests gradual decline, extending the rot metaphor.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Decays\" and \"breaks\" have the same feel, so it is just variety.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Decays" suggests gradual decline, extending the rot metaphor.$q$, $q$"Decays" continues the image of fruit spoiling slowly, while "breaks" suggests a sudden, single event, the kind of deliberate failure the author says rarely happens. Nothing in the passage says decayed links are easier to fix.$q$, 'draft', '2c939315-9602-56db-a4ae-170c85dba003')
