  ('f371b7f8-07bf-5f54-a71b-47b7dff1fb40', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The passage ends by saying that "a source that cannot be found is, for most readers, a source that does not exist." What is the role of this statement?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It stresses that being unreachable has the same practical effect as being gone.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It blames readers for being too lazy to search harder for old sources.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It claims that sources behind broken links were never real to begin with.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It argues that archives are useless because they cannot save every page.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It stresses that being unreachable has the same practical effect as being gone.$q$, $q$The qualifier "for most readers" shows the author means the practical result, not that the source was fake, and this justifies the archiving effort described just before. The author calls the effort worthwhile despite flaws, so the passage does not call archives useless.$q$, 'draft', '2c939315-9602-56db-a4ae-170c85dba003')
